hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  in Chapter 6 of his book language truth  and logic a jair is going to set out  what has come to be called an emotivist  theory of ethics and the version of it  that a Jair has he's treating it as a  sort of meta ethic that is a higher  level discourse that allows us to  account for what's going on and what  people think is going on in ethics and  why it's not actually what it is that  they think is going on in a way you  could say the air is trying to provide  us with an analysis from a higher level  that shows us why all of the people  putting forth ethical theories and  disagreeing with each other are not just  in in some respect wrong but off base  that they're they're talking past each  other and they're misunderstanding their  subjects so emotivism claims to provide  a view on ethical statements theories  expressions whatever it is that you want  to say anything that has to do with  value in a moral sense applying to our  actions or words it claims to provide  some sort of analysis of this that will  show what's really going on  and for air and for other emotivist this  has to do with our emotions or desires  preferences he also uses the term Moral  Sentiments at one point so the things  that we feel about about ethics or about  right and wrong good and bad all of  those sorts of feelings that we have  that that have come to us primarily  through our upbringing and background  and perhaps through some education as  well  now emotivism is not just a meta ethic  sort of above the fray of ethics it also  does represent a more substantive view  on ethics and a sort of bottom level  giving us some ideas about how we ought  to approach things so it's not totally  mad ethical but but eras version of it  remains primarily there and we should  begin by looking at this framework that  he's setting out and there's this  wonderful sentence before that we should  remind ourselves of what he's been doing  in the rest of the chapter so he's been  examining other major theories of ethics  and saying why it is that they don't do  what they're supposed to be doing  looking at the distinction between fact  and value and examining how it is that  we use language in ethical judgments  statements propositions so this ear will  go on and say that in every case so this  is unequivocally this is categorically  in every case in which one would  commonly be said to be making an ethical  judgment of any sort right the function  of the relevant ethical word is purely  emotive notice he's not saying that the  entire statement itself has a function  that's purely emotive there could be a  lot of factual information which might  be verifiable or might not that that  could certainly be  propositional in the other sense you  know able to be true or false but the  ethical component of it the ethical word  or words its function he's saying is  purely emotive now what does it mean to  have an emotive function there's a  number of different sub functions  entailed within what he's calling the  emotive function so going back to his  own words he says it's used to express  feeling about certain objects but not to  make any assertion of them so w